{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:premortem","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:568a71b61d646dd73644f7ec7b1e65406ad20671e83a239c07de4a0375558bab","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.1},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.1}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Wave 1 P50s: 2.00 / 1.60 / 1.80. I am at 2.10, above all three, with a fatter right tail than any of them (P90 3.1 vs 2.95 / 2.60 / 2.90).\n\nTHE DOUBLE-COUNT. All three subtracted 2A's locked aging drag from a convergence baseline, and P2 made the error explicit and then certified it: \"a no-aging China counterfactual of ~4.9% minus 2.7 = 2.2%. The two routes agree.\" They do not independently agree; they are the same subtraction twice. The IMF's -1.1 pp global / -2.7 pp China is measured against a NO-AGING counterfactual, whereas realized 2026 growth of 4.4-4.5% (P3's own observed datum) already embeds the drag that has been running since China's working-age peak in 2012. Deducting it again from a hypothetical 4.9% is a construction, not an observation. P1 did the same from \"a ~5% base\" and landed at 2.5-3.0%; P2 landed at 2.2-2.4%, which is below every identified medium-term path for China. The defensible China figure for 2032-36 is convergence-plus-aging taken ONCE: 3.0-3.4%. I use 3.2%.\n\nTHE UNPRICED PULL. 2A's baseline is stated \"absent productivity offsets,\" so crediting the locked healthy-aging result (\"70 in 2022 = 53 in 2000,\" 41 countries, N ~ 1M, ~+0.4 pp/yr to global growth 2025-2050) is reading the prior as written, not double-counting it — P2 took +0.15 and P3 \"credit[ed] only part,\" both on a double-counting worry that the prior's own wording forecloses. Two further mechanical channels got zero from all three: a shrinking labour force sheds its LOWEST-productivity marginal workers first, raising measured output per hour with no technology involved; and capital deepening per worker is forced rather than chosen when labour is the binding factor.\n\nWEIGHTS: OECD-38 PPP ~$78-82T, China ~$41T in 2025, so China is ~0.33 of the combined aggregate, drifting to ~0.34 by 2032. I use 0.66 / 0.34. Note P2's cross-check against the model's 0.629/0.371 is contaminated — those weights are outputs of a gamma=0.55 useful-energy production function in which China's faster electricity build mechanically inflates its GDP level, so the model's China weight is biased HIGH and the check is not independent of the quantity being checked.\n\nOECD-38 2032-36 BUILD: +0.60 locked per-capita baseline (absent offsets) +0.20 population (migration-driven) = 0.80 baseline; +0.35 AI TFP offset (AI-Q6's own regime probabilities give 0.162 x 0.75 + 0.472 x 0.35 + 0.365 x 0.06 = 0.309, and I take it close to face value rather than haircutting it as P2 did, because the Hulten/Baumol haircut he applied assumes diffusion lands in input-measured public sectors — an assumption, not an observation, and one the labour-scarcity demand pull argues against, since the binding shortages are in logistics, construction and utilities too); +0.30 participation/healthy-aging; +0.10 marginal-worker composition; +0.12 capital deepening net of AI-Q4's impairment prior; -0.15 frictions (interest reallocation, interconnect queues, in-window climate damages, which are genuinely small). = 1.52. Use 1.5.\nCHINA 2032-36: 3.2 (see above).\nP50 = 0.66 x 1.5 + 0.34 x 3.2 = 0.99 + 1.088 = 2.08 -> 2.1.\n\nP10 = 1.0: the lower-tail premortem. 2028-30 impairment synchronous with a sovereign funding accident, pro-cyclical consolidation, BIS Box C demand deficit realized, China balance-sheet recession. 0.66 x 0.5 + 0.34 x 1.7 = 0.33 + 0.58 = 0.91, rounded to 1.0 to respect P1's correct observation that no 5-year window of this aggregate since 1960 has printed below ~1.8% and that secular-stagnation calls have systematically overshot the downside.\nP90 = 3.1: the upper-tail premortem. Permitting reform forced by the fiscal squeeze, electricity supply overshooting demand from 2031, adoption driven by the unfillable vacancy rather than by capability and landing in MARKET-measured sectors so it registers as TFP, participation extension running ahead of the healthy-aging prior, China's property stock adjustment completing by 2030 plus a consumption-share pivot. 0.66 x 2.4 + 0.34 x 4.3 = 1.58 + 1.46 = 3.05 -> 3.1. Wave 1 truncated this tail on the grounds that \"the demographic denominators are near-deterministic over 10 years\"; that is true of the population projection and false of labour INPUT and of output per unit of it, which is where all the variance lives.\nMild RIGHT skew — the opposite of P1's left-skew call — because the deterministic part of the demographics is in the denominator while the uncertain part (participation, hours, measured productivity, Chinese policy) is in the numerator.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:57.080Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"US core PCE 12-month inflation prints at or below 2.2% for three consecutive monthly BEA releases, AND the euro-area HICP core 12-month rate is at or below 2.0% in the same quarter, with neither driven by a measured recession (OECD-38 harmonized unemployment still under 6%).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills my central claim that the cold leg is mismeasured and the hot legs persist. My CQ2 conjunct A falls from 0.72 to ~0.45 and CQ2 drops from 35 to ~22; CQ6-C falls from 26 to ~15, CQ6-D rises from 10 to ~18, CQ6-B absorbs the rest (~43). Hypothesis plank (c) rises from 32 to ~60, i.e. all the way back to the Wave-1 centre. Does not move CQ3 or CQ6-A.\"